WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.204
Request controls are broader than rate limiting.

2
00:00:03.204 --> 00:00:04.540
ASP.

3
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:16.690
NET Core applications also depend on server-side controls that limit request size, connection behavior, header processing, body transfer behavior, and protocol-specific resource use.

4
00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:22.965
These controls help protect the application before a request reaches expensive business logic.

5
00:00:22.965 --> 00:00:29.240
Kestrel request body size limits help control how large a request body the server will accept.

6
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.314
This matters for APIs, file uploads, import endpoints, and form submissions.

7
00:00:34.314 --> 00:00:37.986
A body limit should match the endpoint's business need.

8
00:00:37.986 --> 00:00:45.263
Unlimited sizes or extremely large defaults can increase memory, disk, CPU, and downstream capacity pressure.

9
00:00:45.263 --> 00:00:52.005
Connection limits protect server capacity by limiting how many active connections the server handles.

10
00:00:52.005 --> 00:00:55.076
Header timeouts help limit slow request setup.

11
00:00:55.076 --> 00:01:02.219
Minimum request body data rates and response data rates provide expectations for progress during transfers.

12
00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:08.962
These controls help reduce stalled or excessively slow requests from tying up resources indefinitely.

13
00:01:08.962 --> 00:01:12.166
HTTP/2-specific limits also matter conceptually.

14
00:01:12.166 --> 00:01:21.246
Header size, stream behavior, and flow-control-related limits can affect how much work the server accepts before application logic runs.

15
00:01:21.246 --> 00:01:31.059
Teams do not need to memorize every setting to make good decisions, but they should know that protocol behavior can influence resource consumption.

16
00:01:31.059 --> 00:01:34.798
Limits should align with expected application use cases.

17
00:01:34.798 --> 00:01:44.144
A public JSON API, internal service endpoint, file upload flow, report download, health check, and admin interface may have different needs.

18
00:01:44.144 --> 00:01:47.014
The right size or timeout is not universal.

19
00:01:47.014 --> 00:01:52.622
It should reflect what the endpoint is meant to do and what the backend can support.

20
00:01:52.622 --> 00:02:01.234
Hosting behind IIS, reverse proxies, gateways, ingress controllers, CDNs, or load balancers can change where limits are enforced.

21
00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:08.444
A request may be rejected before Kestrel sees it, or Kestrel may accept work that an upstream layer allowed.

22
00:02:08.444 --> 00:02:13.918
Production review should map which layer owns each limit and how the layers align.
